ASSET TYPES, PREVIEW AND LOCAL DISTRIBUTION
Design note — v0.1 — 2026-09-29
Status: PARTLY BUILT — see the divergence table in section 4. The check set,
the download, and PREVIEW for images and video exist (library and approval
queue, TODO 89); upload types, model preview and format compliance do not.
Decisions still open are marked in section 6.

================================================================================
1. WHAT THIS SECTION COVERS
================================================================================

Three related capabilities, raised together but separable:

  A. Upload types — Blender, Nuke, Unreal, Maya, images. Per-type rules,
     validation and handling. Today `asset_type` is free text. FILE CONTENTS ARE
     INSPECTED — `content_matches_extension` reads the first 512 bytes and
     matches them against a format signature table (shared/assetRules.mjs), so a
     JPEG renamed .png is caught. What does NOT exist is FORMAT COMPLIANCE:
     polygon counts, texture dimensions, naming conventions.

  B. Preview — seeing the asset on the website, so an approver can judge it
     without downloading.

     BUILT 2026-10-29 for IMAGES AND VIDEO (TODO 89): the approval queue and
     the library show a 320px WebP, images through sharp and stills it cannot
     read (TGA, EXR) through ffmpeg; a video's preview is a frame at 1s.
     Nothing here yet for models — see section 5 C.

  C. Download to the approver's machine — where they have a working copy of
     the game in development, so they can review the asset in situ.

The immediate driver: the approval workflow was reachable (Phase 1) but
BLIND. An approver saw an asset name, a version note, a file list with sizes and
hashes — and could not look at the actual bytes. Reviewing a submission was an
act of faith. See TODO item 6.

  CORRECTED 2026-10-29: no longer blind for images and video. The queue shows
  a thumbnail of every one (TODO 89), scoped to the approval. It is still blind
  for models and scene files, which is section 5 C's remaining work.

